    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000115"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The external sac of skin that encloses the testes. It is an extension of the abdomen, and in placentals is located between the penis and anus.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000412"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:uberon.owl</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000007"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>scrotal</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In elephants the testes remain in the abdomen</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In hedgehogs the testes remain in the inguinal canal</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In humans and some other mammals, the base of the scrotum becomes covered with pubic hair at puberty</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In marsupials, the scrotum is not homologous to the eutherian scrotum, and development is under the control of X-linked genes, not androgens</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In marsupials, the scrotum is positioned in the caudal area of the abdomen, in the same position as the pouch in females. It has been suggested that the pouch and marsupial scrotum are sexual homologs, but it is more likely they arise from different primordia in the same morphogenetic field</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:UBPROP_0000008"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001300</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The scrotum of marsupials is not homologous with the scrotum of mammals[ISBN:113945742X]</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
