        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5212832</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Pericardium 8</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasVasculatureDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5212832</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The Common palmar digital artery.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5212832</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>PC 8</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 10, Sec. III, The Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasIndicationsDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Knee pain, weakness of the lower extremities.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasLocationalDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>A Xiyan point located in the depression, lateral to the patellar ligament, locating the point with the knee flexed. Lateral Xiyan overlaps with Dubi (ST 35).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMethodDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5- 1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001465</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0003676</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSurfaceLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001465</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5220479</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Lateral Xiyan</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 95. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasChineseLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>天窗(窓)</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasIndicationsDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Sore throat, sudden loss of voice, deafness, tinnitus, stiffness and pain of the neck.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasInnervationDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The cutaneous cervical nerve, the emerging portion of the great auricular nerve.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasLocationalDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>In the anterior region of the neck, posterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle, at the same level as the superior border of the thyroid cartilage. Note 1: The sternocleidomastoid muscle is more distinct when the head is turned to the opposite side against resistance. Note 2: SI16 is located at the same level as St9, LI18 and the superior border of the thyroid cartilage. St9 is located anterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle, SI16, posterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and LI18, midway between the anterior and posterior borders of the sternocleidomastoid muscle.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMeridian"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9093316</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMethodDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Puncture perpendicularly 0.3-0.7 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasPinyinLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Tianchuang</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0000974</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001128</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001738</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSurfaceLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0000974</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSynonym"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Small Intestine 16</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasVasculatureDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The ascending cervical artery.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5246417</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>SI 16</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 187. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasChineseLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>風(风)市</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasIndicationsDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Pain and soreness in the thigh and lumbar region, paralysis of the lower limbs, beriberi, general pruritus.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasInnervationDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve, the muscular branch of the femoral nerve.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasLocationalDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>On the lateral aspect of the thigh, in the depression posterior to the iliotibial band where the tip of the middle finger rests, when standing up with the arms hanging alongside the thigh. Note: GB31 is located by first finding the iliotibial band when the knee is slightly flexed and the hip is abducted against resis- tance.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMeridian"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5283975</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMethodDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Puncture perpendicularly 0.7-1.2 inches. Moxibustion is applicable.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasPinyinLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Fengshi</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0000376</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <IRI>http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795324</IRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSurfaceLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0000376</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSynonym"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>G 31</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSynonym"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Gallbladder 31</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasVasculatureDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The muscular branches of the lateral circumflex femoral artery and vein.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5264783</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>GB 31</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dc:description"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5282844</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>An acupuncture point that is located along the Stomach Meridian of Foot-Yangming (ST), which consists of 45 standardized acupoints beginning at ST 1 (Chengqi) on the face between the eyeball and the infraorbital margin directly below the pupil, traveling down across the face, neck, chest, and abdomen and continuing along the anterior-lateral aspect of the leg, and ending at ST 45 (Lidui) on the lateral side of the second toe near the corner of the nail.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5282844</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMeridian"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5282844</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_6221269</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSynonym"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5282844</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Acupoint of the stomach meridian of foot-yangming</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasSynonym"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_5282844</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Acupuncture point of the stomach meridian</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
