        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9695467</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Crossing Point Role</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dc:description"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9719785</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Dazhu (BL II) is the Influential Point of bone</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9719785</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9719785</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_3049719</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9719785</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Influential Point of Bone</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dc:description"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9763906</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The Eight Influential Points refer to the specific points which have particular effects in treatment of disorders relating to the zang-fu organs, qi, blood, tendon, pulse and vessels, bone and marrow. Each of the Eight Influential Points overlaps the other point. Geshu (BL17) is the Influential Point of blood.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9763906</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasDesignatedSpecialPointRole"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9763906</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_3965890</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9763906</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Influential Point of Blood</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="dcterms:bibliographicCitation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Source for the Acupoint Location: WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization. Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 82. ISBN 978-92-9061-248-7.

Source for Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, and Indications:
D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Chapter 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridian. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasChineseLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>靑(青)靈(灵,霊)</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasIndicationsDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Pain in the cardiac and hypochondriac regions, shoulder and arm.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasInnervationDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve, the medial brachial cutaneous nerve and the ulnar nerve.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasLocationalDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>On the medial aspect of the arm, just medial to the biceps brachii muscle, 3 B-cun superior to the cubital crease. Note: With the elbow flexed and the arm abducted, Ht2 is located at the junction of the upper two thirds and lower one third of the line connecting Ht1 with Ht3.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMeridian"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9474009</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasMethodDescription"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Puncture perpendicularly 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasPinyinLabel"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>Qingling</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001460</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="TARA:hasRelatedLocation"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>TARA:TARA_9830532</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:UBERON_0001507</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
